Subject: [PATCH 11347/11362] docs(arc): the 27B does not submit before the
 closure compels it

Trial 2, appended to the trial-1 note. Round-2 grammar, force turn at the
loop's own default of 3 instead of trial 1's hardcoded 2, so the model had
three free turns rather than two.

Result: first submission at turn 3 in 7 of 7 grammar cells, at no earlier turn
in any of them. Twenty-one free turns across the arm, zero submissions. Given
the extra free turn the model spent it on more inspection every time --
query_region in four games, a second diff_grids in three.

The design leaned toward the positive: more room to submit than trial 1 gave,
not less. The negative arrived against that lean, and the falsifier was
available and simple (any cell with first_submission_turn < 3 and zero nudges).

So the grammar's demonstrated value is transport efficiency, not autonomous
tool use. Same held-out mean as the control (0.19) for 2,980 decode tokens
against 28,027, and 175 s against 810. That is a real result and it is not the
one a reader would assume from "the model uses the tools".

Both round-2 changes verified model-free against llama.cpp's test-gbnf-validator
before any GPU: 24 candidates x 2 grammars, 48 verdicts, 0 disagreements. Three
first-pass disagreements were all the checker, not the grammar, and the cases
that exposed them are kept.

The grammar freeze was measured on the wire rather than read from the source:
every cell carried exactly the two expected grammar hashes, full on turns 0-2
and submit-only on turn 3. The check fails closed.

Control 7/7, reproducing trial 1 to the token. GPU wall 16.8 min on GPU 1,
server stopped by exact PID, both cards released.

